Module dorothea "The Dorothea" {
  // ── How the passage to Skerrow ends ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  // A voyage ends when the Dorothea is home from the island she was bound for. What it produced is what the island
  // is left with: a lamp lit and unasked about, a lamp kept against the factor, a bronze sold, a boy lost, an engine
  // fed or stilled. Every one of these is made by an action a player can see. The more particular is written first.
  // The other calls (Port Ness, the Antlers, Ashkeld, the Mangrow) end nothing: she is turned round for the next.
  Query fromSkerrow() means safeHome(hull) and bound(hull) == 1.

  Ending end_green "The Green Light" when fromSkerrow() and pilotLit(party) and not lampOut(party)
    "On the second night out, with the Combs a day astern, a green light comes up on the rim of the sea and stays. Bascom is at the wheel. She has steered by one dead star and the memory of a reef for twenty years, and she says nothing at all; she corrects the helm by a point and takes the pipe out of her mouth. Nipper wants to know what it is. 'A thing somebody fed,' says Rune, and for once nobody tells him to hold his tongue. By the end of the week the Tolleth boats are setting their courses by it, and not one of them could say who they thank.".
  Ending end_green_bare "A Light With No Tower" when fromSkerrow() and pilotLit(party) and lampOut(party)
    "The green comes up in the first dark, not from the hill but from under it, a pallor in the water as if a lamp had been lit at the bottom of the sea. There is no reflector left on Skerrow to throw it. Bascom watches it off the quarter for an hour and says that a light that points at nothing is a hard thing to be grateful to. The Reach will learn to look down. On the hill Marren Kestle has nothing to polish, and under her feet the engine, fed, goes on with its counting.".
  Ending end_second_gun "The Second Gun" when fromSkerrow() and reachPaid(party) and not pilotLit(party)
    "Two guns, a night apart, and no third. Wherever you were on the Reach when they went, Tolleth had the name before you did. Dacey Hoe has pinned it up under the others on the harbourmaster's door, in a hand that is trying to be even, and when you stop to read it she does not look up. Bascom tells off her casks twice. On Skerrow the lamp room is dark, and a woman sits in it with her back to the glass. She was asked once what she would take for it. Nobody has asked her since what it was for.".
  Ending end_dark_hour "A Dark Hour" when fromSkerrow() and brakeThrown(party) and not pilotLit(party)
    "You left the Pharos dark, with the pilot unfed and the engine standing in its own silence under the hill, and Marren Kestle with her back to it. Whether the Reach paid, you will learn at Tolleth, from the first boat you meet. You find you have no wish to meet one. Bascom takes the Dorothea home on the ebb with all her lamps lit and none of them any use to anyone but her.".
  Ending end_net_on_table "The Net on the Table" when fromSkerrow() and lampOut(party) and wenLost(party)
    "The plate went down the hill on a barrow, ringing, and Jory Kestle was paid sixty down against the winter. In the morning the island's second boat was found on the black rocks with eleven pots in her, and nobody in her. Hester Gull has written a line on the slate in her own hand and left the space for the name empty. The Dorothea goes home with Orsett's reflector crated under tarpaulin in her hold, and Bascom, who counts everything, does not count it.".
  Ending end_plate_sold "Sixty Down" when fromSkerrow() and lampOut(party)
    "The Dorothea goes home with a crated reflector in her hold and Silas Quillon on the quarterdeck, shaking hands with the crew one by one; it is the pleasantest passage she has made. On the hill the lamp room is a rectangle of darker dark. Down the hill, Jory Kestle is mending a net in a house where four children are warm and the boy came in by a torch on the jetty. Both of these are true. Nobody aboard tries to make them one.".
  Ending end_lens "Tell Them It Was Kept" when fromSkerrow() and keptLamp(party) and not lampOut(party) and lensGiven(kestle)
    "The lamp burns on the hill at Skerrow, and it does not throw as far as it did. Marren Kestle stands in the door of the tower and does not wave, and then does. You have the green glass against your chest in its marked ring, warm, and not yours. 'Tell them it was kept the whole time,' she said, on the stair. It was. That is the thing you carry, and it is heavier than the glass.".
  Ending end_kept "What the Lamp Is For" when fromSkerrow() and keptLamp(party) and not lampOut(party)
    "Silas Quillon has taken the last place on the Dorothea and has not opened his ledger since the green. He tells Bascom, pleasantly, that he counted wrong, and Bascom, who is in debt to his employer, says that it happens. Behind you the Pharos is lit in broad day, for nobody, as it was lit before the roads. Jory Kestle will have a hard winter and a sister who speaks to him. Nobody on Skerrow has been asked what the lamp is for. Nipper, who was not asked either, says at the mouth of the roads that he thinks he knows.".
  Ending end_unasked "The Tower on the Hill" when fromSkerrow()
    "You came, you saw the tower and the sheep and a man at a ledger, and you went home on the ebb. Nobody asked you anything the island could not do without you, and so the island decided, as islands do, in its own time, in February. The lamp was lit when you left. Whether it will be lit when the Dorothea next sails is a question you have left with Hester Gull, who has written it down.".
}
